# ionix-lock: the password check. Read through PamContext.configDirectory, so
# it never has to be installed into /etc/pam.d.
#
# Deliberately pam_unix alone rather than `auth include login`: that stack
# pulls in pam_faillock, which after three typos locks the account for ten
# minutes — at a lock screen that is a lockout from your own desktop, not
# protection. pam_unix's own delay after a failure still applies.
#
# pam_gnome_keyring then unlocks the `login` keyring with the same password.
# The tty1 autologin hands PAM no password, so on the `locked` login mode this
# lock is the only place the keyring can learn it. The module finds the
# session's daemon through $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/keyring/control and talks to it
# directly, since the lock already runs as that user; with no daemon, or a
# keyring under another password, it fails and `optional` ignores that.
# pam_unix is `requisite` so a mistyped password never reaches it.
auth     requisite pam_unix.so
auth     optional  pam_gnome_keyring.so
account  required  pam_unix.so
